WEBVTT

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:03.290
You

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:36.830
it's too ridiculous I haven't been to the seaside for years and now instead of

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:41.150
doing whatever it is one does at the seaside here I am sleuthing like mad

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:46.580
trying to clear my poor husband's name I mean it's potty isn't it look around you

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:53.700
would anyone think that 24 hours ago a perfectly strange young girl was found

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:59.980
dead in our library and now here I am with you tracking down suspects at a five

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:10.200
star seaside hotel murder of the majestic i'm sorry jane were you thinking what oh no well perhaps

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:29.310
yes ah well then i'll be quiet jane what is it a tube yes i don't know

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.940
It's just a tune.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:55.150
I suppose the key to it all was that eight years of mourning were coming to an end.

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.470
I've only just realised it.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:01.950
Thanks for you, Andy.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:03.700
Me?

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.180
Yes, if you hadn't struggled free of me a little this holiday,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:09.560
then I doubt...

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:13.330
Oh, God.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:15.720
A little ruby.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.580
Yes, I'm sorry, Mr. Jefferson.

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.910
We policemen can rarely leave at decent interval.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:29.910
Quiet.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:34.590
But I want to help Chief Constable, very much.

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:36.570
What do you want me to tell you?

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.680
Well, as I understand it, eight years ago in the plane crash,

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:44.750
you lost not only your wife, but both your children, a son and a daughter.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.580
And they each left behind a wife and a husband,

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:52.380
namely Mrs. Jefferson here and Mr. Mark Gaskell.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:56.630
Yes, we were very much thrown together, as you might imagine.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:03:59.960
Mark and Adelaide were very good to meet.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.640
I sometimes think I owe my will to live to them.

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:10.600
I've become more independent, mind you, over the years.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.460
Perhaps it was necessary.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:24.820
If you'll excuse me, Colonel, I really ought to find my son, Peter.

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:26.030
Yes, of course.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:28.600
I'll see you soon, Geoff.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.250
Mr. Jefferson, when you announced that you intended to adopt Ruby Keane as your daughter,

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:47.270
what was the reaction of Mrs. Jefferson and Mark Gaskell?

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.980
They weren't tremendously pleased, but they took it all right.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.280
And was that the reaction that you expected?

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:58.990
It was, yes. I didn't expect them to like it.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.860
I had made healthy provision for both of them when they married my children.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:05.220
Yes.

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.460
So I didn't expect them to behave unreasonably.

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:12.170
It's my experience that families often do.

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:15.980
But we shared a lot.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:17.380
Don't forget.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.680
And they had no blood claim on me.

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:21.900
True.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:25.570
And when you decided to adopt Ruby Keane,

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.170
did you also tell them how much you were going to leave her?

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:29.500
They're not stupid.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:32.480
They'd have made educated guesses.

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:36.070
How much was it, in fact?

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:38.320
A hundred thousand.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:42.680
Yes, I suppose that's a motive.

00:05:45.870 --> 00:05:50.880
I don't envy you trying to prove either Mark or Adelaide a murderer.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:18.070
Jane, are you going to tell me or aren't you?

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:19.640
I'm sorry.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:22.960
For heaven's sake, who strangled Ruby Keaton?

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.320
And why did they leave her body in our life?

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:27.820
Ooh, ooh, that, yeah.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:29.880
Well, isn't that what you've been thinking?

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:40.740
Anyway, yes, of course it's obvious who might have done it, but they say there's something missing, yes.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:04.120
As soon as we come up the red line to HQ. Oh, and cars on standby 24 hours a day.

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.960
Inspector Slacks table there. Backup table's here. Let's get on with them.

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:13.360
Ah, yes, Mr Bartley. Ruby Keane seemed happy enough to dance with you.

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:17.390
Hadn't thought of that, yeah. Well, sort of a job, really, isn't it?

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:21.050
Yeah, I think she was quite, um, I danced quite decently, you know.

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:24.510
You finished dancing when? Gosh, sorry. Couldn't say.

00:07:24.890 --> 00:07:28.280
No idea at all. Sorry. It's a thing in time, isn't it?

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:29.740
What did you do after you stopped dancing?

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.860
Well, you know how it is. Thought I might get lucky, take some for a spin, you know.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:36.040
But then she pushed off. Who did?

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:37.900
Ruby, after I last danced.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.180
Lake? Yawning like a monkey through most of it, anyway.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:45.640
Then she said she had a headache. I say, was I the last one to see her?

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:48.890
I mean, apart from... Where did she go? To her room, she said.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.620
What did you do? Do? Yeah, see what you mean.

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:55.880
Could have been anything, really. What was it, in fact?

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:59.540
Drink, probably. Usually drink after waggling a leg.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.790
I think I wandered about and stowed about outside.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:04.320
Hate it when it's stuffy.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:04.700
Hate?

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:06.400
Of course, that's it.

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:08.280
Must have, because that's when I spotted it.

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:08.870
What?

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:11.010
Well, I didn't spot it because it wasn't there.

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:13.010
Sorry, but I spotted it had gone.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:13.470
What?

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:14.270
The motor.

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:15.450
Your car?

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:16.220
Absolutely.

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:20.390
Sorry, I reported it with the chaps down at the station at Thingamere Road.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:22.320
I say, you couldn't, could you?

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:26.410
I mean, you seem to be a little more intelligent than those chaps down there.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:29.970
It's a Vauxhall coaster, red with...

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.860
Sorry, Mr. Bartley, that was a murder inquiry.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.540
Oh, yeah. Sorry. Go on. Yeah. Forgot. Sorry.

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.770
I'm sorry to trouble you again.

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:20.550
Not at all.

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:22.170
We need to establish...

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:23.120
Blake!

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:23.880
Sir.

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:26.530
That should be so good as to...

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:27.210
Hello.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:29.190
Yes, can I help?

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:31.100
Ackroyd.

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:33.750
Mrs. Perkins.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.130
Yes, now, what can I do?

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:35.970
Now, Miss Turner.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:39.770
We need to establish the actual times, you see,

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:42.330
of your cousin's movements, and, of course,

00:09:42.410 --> 00:09:44.070
the movements of others who are involved.

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:45.050
Yes, of course.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.140
Now, you said in your previous statement

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.100
that she did her exhibition dance with Mr. Starr.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:54.300
Yes, that was 10.30, there or thereabouts.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:56.520
She used to look forward to it.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.100
Ladies and gentlemen, Miss Ruby Keene!

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:27.100
Mark? Sorry. Sorry for writing letters.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:29.820
Come on, Mark. Ruby's just starting to dance.

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:49.890
It was just after that that Ray came over to the table.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:51.290
He wasn't pleased.

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:02.470
I'm sorry to interrupt.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:03.760
Not at all.

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:05.380
As long as you don't change the run of the cards.

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:06.440
As long as you do.

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:08.650
Josie, Ruby hasn't turned up.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:10.990
We were supposed to be on again five minutes ago.

00:12:11.380 --> 00:12:13.180
She was dancing with poor little rich boys.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:15.280
George Bartlett hasn't seen her for half an hour.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:16.420
She isn't in her room.

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:18.720
I wish she wouldn't do this sort of thing.

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:20.040
Excuse me for a moment, will you?

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:20.940
I am sorry.

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:29.460
But we're supposed to be on now.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:32.600
But she's your responsibility. Where the hell is she?

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:33.900
Bridget, have you seen Ruby?

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:37.560
Listen, Ray, we'll have to do a toned-down version ourselves.

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:39.880
Right, I'll buzz upstairs and change.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:42.860
You work out a routine with plenty of the slow spectacular,

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:44.800
not too much of the athletic flash.

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:48.640
You'll probably put that ankle right back.

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:15.280
Until nearly two. Mr. Starr persuaded me to go to bed eventually.

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:18.530
When did the bridge game actually begin?

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:23.040
10.30. We would have started earlier, but Mr. Gaskell had some letters to write.

00:13:23.370 --> 00:13:26.800
At least, that's what he said. I think it was an excuse, actually.

00:13:27.220 --> 00:13:28.920
He's not that keen on bridge, I don't reckon.

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:29.980
But he was back in...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:36.010
time to see Ruby's dance? Yes. And did anyone leave the table before Ruby was

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:43.240
reported missing? No. I knew what I was doing. Others will tell you that Ruby

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:50.850
amused me. It was more than that. To an extent, of course, she was performing. She

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:58.040
was a natural storyteller. She heard that from her background. Her parents were both

00:13:58.680 --> 00:13:59.100
theatricals.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:04.770
What a childhood. Dreadful lodgings.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:08.700
Catching the train on Sundays and not going to church.

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:11.380
They had to get to the next touring date, you see.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:15.510
Fascinating to me, this sort of thing.

00:14:17.340 --> 00:14:21.120
And you know, even the most horrifying anecdotes always ended in laughter.

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:26.770
A laughter, I mean. No complaints, no comments.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:29.680
Just clear-headed amusement.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:38.850
She had a marvellous knowledge of life, and it had left her unsullied.

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:41.670
Unsullied?

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.760
Yes. I want to make that clear.

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:15.490
For sale 100, Whitney Markham.

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:23.690
Excuse me, have you talked to Sir Henry yet?

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:25.440
No, sir.

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:26.340
Why the hell not?

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:28.320
You're a bloody old woman, Edwards.

00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:29.970
I told you...

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:36.470
it was urgent I haven't spoken to Sir Henry sir because he was not at home I did however leave a

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:41.880
message with his housekeeper who said she could contact him I have no doubt that he will be here

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:47.250
for lunch as requested and if you'll excuse me sir I shall now go and confirm the arrangements

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:05.540
I'm Peter Carmody. My name's not Jefferson, you see, because Mummy was married before.

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:07.610
My real daddy was killed in the war.

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:08.110
Gotcha.

00:16:09.100 --> 00:16:11.780
Do you like detective stories? I do most of them.

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:16.370
Got lots of autographs. Dixon Carr, H.C. Bailey and Dorothy Sayers.

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:18.160
Will the murder be in the papers?

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:18.560
Well?

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:22.760
Because I'm going back to school next week and I'll be able to tell the chaps as I knew her.

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:24.280
I mean, really knew her well.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:24.980
You did, did you?

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:25.720
Yes.

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:26.660
What did you think about her?

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:29.780
Not much, quite honestly. She was pretty thick.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:33.520
Mummy and Uncle Mark didn't like her much, either. Only grandfather.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:36.720
She was always butting in. They're glad she's dead.

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:37.340
How do you know?

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:40.980
Uncle Mark said it was one way out anyway, and Mummy said yes.

00:16:41.180 --> 00:16:42.960
But it was so horrible or something,

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:45.720
and Uncle Mark said he didn't see the point in being hypocritical.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:56.420
Sir, I thought I should take a look at Ruby Keane's room for myself.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.410
Of course, sir. Your prerogative.

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:02.370
That was Mrs. Jefferson's boy, wasn't it?

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:02.730
Mm-hmm.

00:17:03.710 --> 00:17:04.890
Did he have anything to say for himself?

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.870
Oh, he thinks his mother probably did it, aided and abetted by his uncle.

00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:09.800
Does he now?

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:16.570
Chambermaids only do the staff rooms once a week, so nothing was touched.

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:18.320
No unaccounted prints.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.640
Is this the dress she was wearing for the exhibition dance?

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:23.520
Yes, sir.

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:27.690
As far as we can tell, she left George Bartlett at half past eleven,

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:29.210
and nobody saw her after that.

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:29.980
So...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.400
Presumably, she came up here, changed out of this into the white dress she was wearing when she was found.

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:35.140
Yes, sir.

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:38.440
Who occupies the other rooms on this floor?

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:41.100
Only Josie Turner opposite. The other rooms are empty.

00:17:41.580 --> 00:17:45.060
The extra summer staff used them later, so there was no one to see her.

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:52.480
The fire escape's hardly lit at night.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.000
She could climb down and practically guarantee not to be noticed.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:59.210
Yes, there's only one reason why a girl changes her dress.

00:17:59.450 --> 00:17:59.750
Quiet!

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:01.230
She was going to meet someone.

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:05.950
I'm sorry you had to cancel your little gathering.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:07.030
I was looking forward to it.

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:08.430
Everyone's catching it.

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:11.680
The nailers rang last night to cancel their doom.

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:14.620
Never mind.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:16.200
Yes, yes.

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:18.670
Well, my regards to Mrs. Duffy.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:20.730
Goodbye.

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:44.740
Come on Malcolm, you said you wanted to see the Colonel, you can't back out now.

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:54.160
You've got to tell. Now come on, here's a good one.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:58.360
Malcolm, how's the world with you?

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:01.950
Malcolm's made a discovery, sir.

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:03.340
Oh, he has.

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:06.840
What?

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:10.430
Found a buddy.

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:41.880
I was going to meet somebody.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:45.800
Now, suppose it was a boyfriend, and he was a bit of a hard case.

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:48.500
Exactly the sort of person she'd want to keep dark,

00:19:48.600 --> 00:19:50.260
so as not to foul up her chances with Jefferson.

00:19:50.540 --> 00:19:52.240
It's hardly surprising. Nobody knew about him.

00:19:53.570 --> 00:19:59.350
Now, suppose she tells him that Jefferson is going to adopt her.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.140
He doesn't like this a bit.

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:03.740
Gonna take her right out of his class.

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:04.640
He's as good as lost her.

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:07.750
He probably doesn't mean to kill her, but he's stupid.

00:20:08.470 --> 00:20:09.450
Doesn't know his own strength.

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:10.980
Panics.

00:20:13.050 --> 00:20:13.850
Rings her neck.

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:16.540
Drives her around a bit.

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:18.630
Hardly knows where he is.

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:21.750
Passes Gossington Hall.

00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:23.690
Big house.

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:25.210
Easy to break into.

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:29.040
Dumps her there to divert attention and clears off to London.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:34.180
Well, I think it's about the best line we have at the moment.

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:37.680
There is the financial motive, of course,

00:20:37.720 --> 00:20:40.700
but at this stage it looks as if Mrs. Jefferson and Gaskell

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.040
have a cast-iron alibi.

00:20:44.060 --> 00:20:45.100
Well, I must be off.

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:58.210
Clack, I want you to report to me at the end of each day,

00:20:58.820 --> 00:20:59.500
whenever you finish.

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:04.060
The Bantry's are neighbors of mine.

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:06.850
Huh?

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:19.010
Tell Prescott, that's the manager,

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.150
that I want to speak to that flash piece of goods who teaches tennis.

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:22.790
What's his name?

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:23.470
Star.

00:21:24.700 --> 00:21:27.380
Budget, hop down to the tennis courts and ask Mr. Star to come up.

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:28.580
You should be finished by now.

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:34.370
There. I'll send Mr. Starr along to the room that we've given you.

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:35.250
Thank you, sir.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:10.580
Can I help you, madam?

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:13.030
See you, madam.

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:14.920
Madam!

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:17.860
I beg your pardon?

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:18.820
How can I help you, madam?

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:20.220
Oh, I only wish you could.

00:22:20.990 --> 00:22:25.050
I sometimes feel we have no control over what our eyes decide to see or not to see.

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:28.370
Oh!

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:29.300
That's it.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:29.700
Yes.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.420
Would you like to buy one of those newspapers?

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:33.420
No, no. Compliments of the house.

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:35.350
Thank you. Thank you very much.

